# Foreword to the 2nd edition (2026)

[M]{custom-style=initial-joined}[ost of what I wrote]{.smallcaps}, in the foreword to the first edition of this book in 2024, still holds true.

I have contributed a few more fixes and improvements to SILE's math engine.
At this date, they are still pending inclusion (expectedly in SILE 0.15.14), and are already available in the _résille_ distribution.[^foreword-resille]
This book has been updated accordingly, with new examples --- particularly for primes, stretched delimiters, and accents.

There is still room for improvement, and using the system remains one of the best ways to uncover edge cases.
After all, most issues have been found simply by throwing equations at it.
That said, contributions have been quiet lately.
A more active exchange would help shape the math engine into something truly robust and broadly useful.
User feedback and real-world usage are invaluable in identifying shortcomings and guiding improvements.

$$`\table[columnalign=left center left]{
&& \int_0^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{e^x - 1} - \frac{1}{x e^x} \right) dx
 = \int_0^{\infty} \frac{e^{-x} + x - 1}{x[e^x -1]} dx \\
&=& \int_0^{\infty} \frac{1}{x[e^x - 1]} \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}x^{m+1}}{(m+1)!} dx
 = \int_0^{\infty} \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}x^m}{(m+1)![e^x -1]} dx \\
&=& \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \int_0^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}x^m}{(m+1)![e^x -1]} dx
 = \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{(m+1)!} \int_0^{\infty} \frac{x^m}{e^x - 1} dx \\
&=& \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{(m+1)!} m!\zeta(m+1)
 = \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{m+1}\zeta(m+1) \\
&=& \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{m+1} \sum_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{m+1}}
 = \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \sum_{n = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{m+1}\frac{1}{n^{m+1}} \\
&=& \sum_{n = 1}^{\infty} \sum_{m = 1}^{\infty} \frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{m+1}\frac{1}{n^{m+1}}
 = \sum_{n = 1}^{\infty} \left[\frac{1}{n} - \log\left(1+\frac{1}{n}\right)\right]
 = \gamma \\
}`

What I find interesting about SILE's approach to math is that it offers an independent implementation of both MathML (without relying on a web stack) and TeX-like math (without using a traditional TeX engine).
There aren't many free and open-source typesetting systems that handle math well.
Rather than competing, these projects have a real opportunity to learn from one another and collectively advance the tools available to the community.

{.pendant}
---

[^foreword-resille]: The [_résille_](https://github.com/Omikhleia/resille){custom-style=link} distribution is a Docker-based digital typesetting system.
It builds on SILE 0.15.13 and extends it with additional components and tools.
Obviously, this version of the present book is produced with the _résille_ distribution.
